  "145": {
    "inputs": {
      "value": "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1> is the female doctor from <Picture 1>, preserving her facial identity, white medical coat, stethoscope, and neat low bun.\n<Subject 2> is the male patient from <Picture 2>, preserving his facial identity, grey sweater layered over a light-blue shirt, and navy trousers.\n<Subject 3> is the consultation-room environment from <Picture 3>, preserving its bright clinical interior, medical desk, chairs, and clean contemporary furnishings.\n\nsummary:\n[reference generation] The target video is a 15-second, 16:9 English-language workplace-comedy scene for a Western audience. Inside <Subject 3>, <Subject 1> conducts a routine consultation with <Subject 2>, whose fear of elevators leads to a dry visual pun and a restrained sitcom reaction.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4]): fully_preserved - the doctor’s identity, white coat, stethoscope, hairstyle, and professional role are retained.\n<Subject 2> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4]): fully_preserved - the patient’s identity, layered grey-and-blue clothing, navy trousers, and ordinary visitor role are retained.\n<Subject 3> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4]): fully_preserved - the bright consultation room, desk layout, seating, medical details, and clinical lighting are retained.\n\ndetailed_description:\nThe target video uses a polished contemporary American workplace-sitcom style in 16:9, with bright naturalistic clinical lighting, precise reaction timing, understated performances, and clean dialogue synchronization. The comedy should feel dry and intelligent rather than exaggerated. Total duration is exactly 15 seconds. No subtitles, captions, or visible joke text.\n\n[Shot 1] A balanced medium two-shot establishes <Subject 3>. <Subject 1> (S1), wearing her white coat and stethoscope, sits upright behind the medical desk with a tablet in one hand. Across from her, <Subject 2> sits slightly hunched, visibly winded, with flushed cheeks and one hand resting on his knee. The doctor studies him for a moment, then asks in calm, professional English with a subtle North American sitcom cadence, <d>[English] How are you coping with your fear of elevators?</d>\n\n[Shot 2] At 00:03.400, cut to a medium close-up of <Subject 2> (S2). A tiny bead of sweat runs down his temple. He takes one final breath, straightens his sweater, and replies with complete sincerity and no awareness that he is making a joke, <d>[English] I'm taking steps to avoid them.</d> He closes his mouth and gives a small, earnest nod. Leave a half-second of silence after “them.”\n\n[Shot 3] At 00:07.000, cut to a close-up of <Subject 1>. She stops writing. Her eyes slowly move from the patient’s sweaty face to his exhausted legs, then back to his face. Without smiling, she lowers the tablet and says, <d>[English] Clearly.</d> A restrained studio-audience chuckle begins beneath her perfectly neutral expression.\n\n[Shot 4] At 00:10.000, cut to a wider side angle showing both characters and the consultation-room doorway. An elevator bell dings somewhere in the corridor. <Subject 2> instantly freezes, grips both armrests, and looks toward the door in alarm. Without turning her head, <Subject 1> calmly points toward an off-screen stairwell. The patient gives her a grateful nod, rises on trembling legs, and hurriedly exits. The doctor resumes writing as the audience laughter swells briefly, then cuts cleanly at 00:15.000.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nQuiet consultation-room ambience with a soft ventilation hum, faint tablet taps, subtle clothing movement, the patient’s light post-exertion breathing, and one crisp off-screen elevator bell at 00:10.200. Dialogue remains clean, intimate, and foregrounded. A tasteful sitcom audience chuckle follows the punchline and grows slightly during the final visual payoff.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nA sparse, playful comedy bed featuring muted upright bass, soft brushed drums, and occasional pizzicato notes at a relaxed tempo. Keep it very low beneath the dialogue. Add one short descending bass flourish when the elevator bell triggers the patient’s reaction, then end on a neat pizzicato button at 00:15.000."
    },
